==English==

===Etymology===
Of uncertain origin. These possibilities have been suggested:
* Irish {{term|sionnachuighim|lang=ga||I play the fox}}.
* Spanish {{term|chanada||lang=es}}, a shortening of {{term|charranada||lang=es|trick", "deceit}}.
* Palatinate German {{term|schinägeln||lang=pfl|to work hard}}, from the peddler's argot term ''Schenigelei'' ("work").
* French {{term|manigance|ces manigances|lang=fr|these fraudulent schemes}}.

* Persian {{term|شیطنت, sheitanat||to fool around for fun}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʃəˈnænəɡɪn/|lang=en}},
* {{audio|shenanigan_us.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|countable|lang=en}} A deceitful confidence trick, or mischief causing discomfort or annoyance.
#: ''I spotted his next '''shenanigan''' — saw it coming — and so avoided being fooled.''
# {{label|en|rare}} {{singular of|shenanigan|lang=en}}

